[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069947 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of completing the square to solve *any* quadratic equation, even if the equation does not factor.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we want to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-8=0\n$$\n\nWe can do so by following the five steps below:\n\n**Step 1.** Move the constant term to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =8 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2})-1 & =8 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-1 & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Isolate the perfect square by adding $1$ to both sides. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x+1| & =3 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5.** Finally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1-3=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-4$ and $x=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5c2a281-aa37-55de-831b-f68a8a0fad3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1628 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070030 :tutorial/content "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+2x+5}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral doesn't resemble any of the standard integrals we have encountered so far.\n\nHowever, notice that we can complete the square in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+5 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+({1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2})-{1}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of ${\\color{red}{4}}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4[(x+1)^{2}/4+1]}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}/4+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+1)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+1)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this now resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arctan u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x+1}{2}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+1)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" :tutorial/id #uuid "fa12f0db-7152-56d9-b243-3e761f5f2786" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079635 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation \n\n$$\nx^2 +  y^2 - 2x - 4y = 4.\n$$\n\nBelieve it or not, this equation actually represents a circle! Its graph is given below:\n\n![](images/5d/5dfea5042d6c84f40a2790a01ee6aafb64433f7422ce88b589cf670d93771e2c.png)\n\n   \n\nClearly, this is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(1,2)$. But how can we determine the center and radius from the given circle equation?\n\nLet's start with the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 +  y^2 - 2x - 4y = 4.\n$$\n\nBy grouping the $x$ and $y$ terms and completing the squares, we can write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nWe carry out the process with the following steps:\n\n**Step 1.** Group the $x$ and $y$ terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y & =4 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** In each group, add the square of half the linear coefficient to both sides of the equation. \n\n- Here, the $x$ coefficient is $-2$, so we add $\\left( \\dfrac{-2}{2} \\right)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1$ to both sides.\n\n- Likewise, the $y$ coefficient is $-4$, so we add $\\left( \\dfrac{-4}{2} \\right)^2 = (-2)^2 = 4$ to both sides.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-4y+4) & =4+1+4 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-4y+4) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we factor each group into a perfect square, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can see that the equation represents a circle centered at $(1,2)$ with radius $3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8743599-a677-56de-8b27-1b1b04f19643" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2440 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088911 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the equation $4x^2+y^2 - 8x - 4y = 8.$ Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb75dab958d3dedba6e6b685b88f393aa6c28c15c2e52b414a30e97fdfa68a9e.png)\n\nClearly, this is an ellipse. To determine the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes, we need to rewrite the given equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ is the center of the ellipse, and $a$ and $b$ are the horizontal and vertical radii of the ellipse, respectively. \n\nWe do this by completing the square. First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-4y & =8 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-8x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-8x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =8 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2x]+{y}^{2}-4y & =8 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1]+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =8 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =8 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8+4+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the ellipse equation is in the standard form, we can tell that the ellipse is centered at $(1,2).$\n\nAlso, since $4 > 2,$ the length of the semi-major axis is $4$, and the length of the semi-minor axis is $2.$\n\nConsequently, the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 4 =8$ and the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2 =4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb46eb8f-5131-55b1-baf7-b712d9dd5a71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3411 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168098 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-10x+34}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can complete the square for the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 10x + 34 &= x^{2} - 10x + (25 - 25) + 34 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 10x + 25) - 25 + 34 \\\\\n&= (x - 5)^{2} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of $9$ in the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 10x + 34}}dx &= \\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{(x - 5)^{2} + 9}} \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{9[\\frac{(x - 5)^{2}}{9} + 1]}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{x - 5}{3})^{2} + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis now looks more familiar! Let\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac {x-5}{3}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = 3\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 10x + 34}}dx &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{x - 5}{3})^{2} + 1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}} \\cdot (3du) \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a basic integral for an inverse hyperbolic function, and we can solve it in one step:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}}du &= \\operatorname{arsinh} u + C \\\\\n&= \\operatorname{arsinh} (\\frac{x - 5}{3}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "59b06095-0ed1-54ec-b486-9618bb3c0704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168099 :tutorial/content "We can also complete the square to integrate hyperbolic *arcsecant*, *arccosecant*, and *arccotangent*.\n\nFor example, let's consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\left(x+3\\right)\\sqrt{-x^2-6x}} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-6x & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+3)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =9-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of $9$ under the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{9-(x+3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3(x+3)\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis now looks more familiar! Let $u = \\dfrac {x+3}{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 3\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a basic integral for an inverse reciprocal hyperbolic function, and we can solve it in one step:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f0c7466-a41d-5101-b125-8e6f68fba3ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5218 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173147 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the parabola \n\n$$\nx^2 - 4x - 8y - 28 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below. How can we find the vertex of the parabola?\n\n![](images/28/2849c732102f52d5f7f2e0f5e29724560d4d7052df80e90fe8d24b95911f5092.png)\n\nRecall that the standard equation of a *vertical* parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. \n\nTo write our equation in standard form, we group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x-8y-28 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =8y+28 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =8y+28 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =8y+32 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =8(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(2)(y-(-4))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = ({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}-4}).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "57e24d89-1be2-5ee0-bdd5-e0d68dbb8f4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4580 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185300 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a\\neq 0.$ We can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-{(\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}-\\frac{c}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a}  = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{b}{2a} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc56cc55-b423-5e0b-aed0-e07bdb86f874" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13276 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Quadratic Formula"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185337 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to complete the square on the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n- \\left(x^2 + 6x+4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x  + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2+ {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+6x+4) & =-((x+3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "50b34082-7844-5fd2-aaaf-8d4a7fe58412" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185352 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of completing the square to solve quadratic equations with leading coefficients. \n\nAs an example, let's solve the following quadratic equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n4x^2-8x+3 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x+3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =-3 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding  $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =-3+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac12$ and $x=\\dfrac32.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d202804-803f-5139-b32a-fca689609abb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6214 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185383 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nAs we've seen, **completing the square** on a quadratic expression means expressing it as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to complete the square on a quadratic expression when the coefficient of the linear term is odd. In these cases, we get a fraction in the final expression.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 5x + \\left(\\dfrac 52\\right)^2\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the large parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+5x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "858a20e8-2e9b-5ab0-ae5f-811c370a9b56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9421 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185384 :tutorial/content "Let's complete the square on the following expression: \n\n$$\nx^2 + 3x + 4\n$$\n\nThe procedure is very similar to the one we encountered when the coefficient of the linear term was even.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+4 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+4 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{16}{4} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dba1a2d3-8092-514c-905c-9f6c001081ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9422 :tutorial/title "Completing the Square"}]
